'''thong''' (thông), ''n.'' [< ME. ''thong'', ''thwong'', ''thwang'', < AS. ''thwang'', ''thwong'' (= Icel. ''thvengr''), thong, latchet, esp. of shoes, < *''thwingan'' (*''thwang'' in pret.), constrain: see ''twinge''.] A long narrow strip of leather; a narrow strap, used as a fastening, a halter, reins, the lash of a whip, the latchet of a shoe, and in many other ways. See cut under ''snow-shoe''.

{{smaller block|Queme quyssewes [cuisses] then, that coyntlych closed<br>His thik thrawen thyȝez, with ''thwonges'' to-tachched.<br>{{right|''Sir Gawayne and the Green Knight'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 579.}}

After cutte that pece into ''thwanges'' smal,<br>Lete it not be brode, but narow as may be.<br>{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 568.}}

A lethern ''thong'' doth serve his wast to girt.<br>{{right|''Times' Whistle'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 27.}}

From the high box they [coachmen] whirl the ''thong'' around,<br>And with the twining lash their shins resound.<br>{{right|''Gay'', Trivia, iii. 37.}}}}

'''thong''' (thông), ''v.'' [< ME. ''thwongen''; < ''thong'', ''n.''] '''I.'''† ''trans.'' To provide, fit, or fasten with a thong.

{{smaller block|''Thongede'' scheon. {{right|''Ancren Riwle'', p. 362.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' 1. To strike with a thong, or with a similar implement, as the lash of a whip.

{{smaller block|She has hit Mrs. Bonnington on the raw place, and smilingly proceeds to ''thong'' again.<br>{{right|''Thackeray'', Lovel the Widower, iv.}}}}
